ke your ideas to  the bag afterwards and try different  things out  also ensuring to utilize different  levels and not always punching at the  same place  all the time this next point is mainly  applicable to those fighting in  competition and that's using  combinations that are unrealistic  or not something that might happen in a  real situation so although we obviously  don't want to have rehearsed routines  that we always fall back on naturally  this is going to happen to an extent  now we all have our go-to techniques no  matter how advanced we are so it pays  for you to train yourself to fall back  on ones that may give you  the opportunity to get the upper hand so  if this point applies to you then do  ensure that you're training some combos  that are naturally going to be quite  realistic  and go with your style of fighting for  instance one of those would be this  simple combo the first three strikes  will cause the opponent to lift their  hands into a covered position leaving an  opening for the fourth body shot to land  in the ribs and subsequently then  leaving them open for the kick  afterwards or  whatever other applicable technique you  want to execute so those were some of  the common mistakes that i've noticed  throughout my time training  that people tend to fall into when using  the heavy bag  and as mentioned before many of these  areas come down to working within that  fine balance and a lot of the mistakes  are actually the opposite of each other  for instance  not enough power and applying constant  power that sweet spot lies right in the  middle and you want to ensure that  you're working within that point  in order to get the most benefits from  your training there's loads of different  things within this topic that i could  have mentioned here  but i just thought these were some of  the most common ones and some of the  most important that i would mention and  highlight  that you want to watch out for in  training and also if you have any ot