d this one can be  quite difficult for people to understand  especially when they're just starting  out  and the reason for that is the seemingly  paradoxical way in which you have to  twist at the waist  push your shoulders forward in order to  apply power so i noticed that when i've  coached people to twist their body more  in punching combos especially when  throwing across for instance when i say  this and explain there's kind of more  that they could be getting out of that  punch they sometimes begin pushing the  bag rather than  snapping the punches off of the back so  the power in the strikes comes from the  transfer of energy throughout the entire  body starting with the feet that  eventually discharges through the fist  at the end so the punches have already  been set into motion about 85 percent  before the final  extension of the arm and this is the  same with kicking techniques as well  although it's less spoken about  if you want to know more about this  topic in depth i did quite an extensive  video on this topic which i will list in  the description below the next part is  about falling into the pitfall of having  the heavy bag as the only thing that  you're using to train yourself  now i think part of the reason for this  is because hitting the bag is really fun  to do  and it also feels great you get a good  workout all of those endorphins are  released and things like that when  you're doing it  but you need to remember that for  martial artists there are a vast array  of different areas to training even pad  work with a partner is more dynamic  because the partner is able to throw  strikes at you to initiate you to move  around when you're training together the  heavy bag is obviously completely  stationary and motionless whereas an  opponent is dynamic and obviously moves  around a lot not only that but they can  also strike back towards you and learn  to read your  patterns so there's just so many  different variables to take into account  even 