erience when you see  people training like this  now this next point is the opposite of  the last one and that's not applying  enough  power into your strikes so there's too  much power and then there's not enough  power remember i spoke about the optimal  balance at the beginning of this video  and a lot of this just comes down to  actually not really trying hard enough  unfortunately  it's natural that if you're not applying  enough power into at least a portion of  the total strikes you execute then your  body isn't going to benefit from the  conditioning that it otherwise would do  if you were doing that  it's something that i've spoken about  countless times in videos on my channel  as well as the bioneer  but really the best way to condition  yourself for an exercise or an activity  is just by doing the activity again and  again and again however with one very  big caveat which is that it needs to be  done with a sufficient level of effort  in which you push yourself regularly  but not constantly remember that balance  otherwise you just remain at the level  you're currently at  i actually think this point is one of  the main reasons that many martial  artists have kind of stagnated  at their current level of skill actually  and that's because they've become  accustomed to training at a certain pace  and then when i train with them they get  tired easily and i'm thinking you know  you're supposed to be a martial artist  why are you not fit enough to do the  activity we're doing  not enough intensity and i wanted to  stay away from using this word in the  previous sections talking about power  because power and intensity are actually  two different things although there is  some crossover in the middle  the way i like to think about intensity  is more kind of like a high level view  or  theme that you carry into all of your  training sessions  now obviously there's many different  ways that you can use the bag and i'm  certainly not saying there's only one  way to use it but i am talking in  general terms of just progression here  so if we think about the word intensity  kind of like a container in which all of  your training takes place and within  that container can be many different  elements  you can be really intense without  putting in any power at all and just  work on volume of strikes and speed for  instance or like i mentioned in the  previous section you can be working more  upon developing power with fewer strikes  concentrating on technique body position  and so on but these are both different  examples of intensity  and the same also goes for too much  intent as well to where the person is  not leaving any room for kind of freedom  of expression  and development of their own style by  this i mean constantly executing strikes  without really considering what you're  doing  and i think this is where people end up  looking a bit robotic and  one-dimensional  something that also ties into this area  is actually not hitting the bag  enough to receive the sufficient  conditioning benefits that you could  otherwise get from it so the strikes  you're doing could be decent in power  and technique etc but if they're so few  and far between as i said you won't be  getting the conditioning you would  otherwise get with greater volume  and this leads nicely on to the next  point which is not getting your weight  behind the strikes or you could say kind  of only using your arms to throw the  punches  it's not really about applying power  always it's learning how to apply power  more like  this can be a weird one because again  there's a certain level of understanding  about the mechanics of striking that's  required if people have very limited  experience  but even those with relatively decent  technique otherwise can also fall victim  to this when going onto a bag for  instance  and that's because of the change in  environment meaning that there's now a  great big bag in front of you to contend  with and you have to adapt your  technique from hitting pads or shadow  boxing in order to  work effectively and this is something  that's actually really important that  i've not seen spoken much about  so a decent pad holder should provide  equal resistance to the force of the  punches that you're throwing but even  with a pad holder there's no way that  their arms are going to obviously be  able to resist your strikes as much as  a hard and a decent quality heavy bag so  there's a lot to contend with there  and this is applicable for everything  you do but especially for hook punches  for instance  the bag's much wider obviously than a  person's head or the position in which  your training partner will hold pads for  you  and so you've got to adapt for these  variables and this can only really be  gained through  experience of the bag and finding out  what works it's also going to be  different depending on your specific  body type  so you'll have to find out the best way  for you personally to apply power but  there are some general rules about this  that are applicable globally to take  into account  if you want a greater understanding  about this topic i made a video about  kinetic linking and the mechanics of  striking so  please check the link in the description  this next point ties in with the  previous one i mentioned  and that's been really one dimensional  with almost little to no extra movement  many people just stand in one position  and execute the same strikes and combos  continually when training on the bag  and at the same pace as well with no  rhythm or variation  so one of the purposes of the bag is  learning to strike at different angles  how to move off center line circle an  opponent and things like that and this  can be developed in numerous ways but  the one i would suggest  is in shadow boxing because obviously  you've got the complete freedom to move  around  with no obstacles in your way to kind of  find out what works and develop your own  style you can then take your ideas to  the bag afterwards and try different  things out  also ensuring to utilize different  levels and not always punching at the  same place  all the time this next point is mainly  applicable to those fighting in  competition and that's using  combinations that are unrealistic  or not something that might happen in a  real situation so although we obviously  don't want to have rehearsed routines  that we always fall back on naturally  this is going to happen to an extent  now we all have our go-to techniques no  matter how advanced we are so it pays  for you to train yourself to fall back  on ones that may give you  the opportunity to get the upper hand so  if this point applies to you then do  ensure that you're training some combos  that are naturally going to be quite  realistic  and go with your style of fighting for  instance one of those would be this  simple combo the first three strikes  will cause the opponent to lift their  hands into a covered position leaving an  opening for the fourth body shot to land  in the ribs and subsequently then  leaving them open for the kick  afterwards or  whatever other applicable technique you  want to execute so those were some of  the common mistakes that i've noticed  throughout my time training  that people tend to fall into when using  the heavy bag  and as mentioned before many of these  areas come down to working within that  fine balance and a lot of the mistakes  are actually the opposite of each other  for instance  not enough power and applying constant  power that sweet spot lies right in the  middle and you want to ensure that  you're working within that point  in order to get the most benefits from  your training there's loads of different  things within this topic that i could  have mentioned here  but i just thought these were some of  the most common ones and some of the  most important that i would mention and  highlight  that you want to watch out for in  training and also if you have any other  ideas for subsequent episodes in the  common mistakes series  please let me know in the comments  thanks very much guys and i will see you  next time


